# Spatex with GeoFrame

Run this notebook from a git checkout of Spatex: it installs Spatex from the checkout (`path:`) and reads its test fixtures.

> **Not run in CI.** GeoFrame is not on Hex yet, so CI does not fetch it.
> Run this notebook locally with a GeoFrame checkout:
> 
>     SPATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH=/path/to/geoframe livebook server notebooks/geoframe.livemd
> 
> or headlessly, to check that every cell works:
> 
>     SPATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH=/path/to/geoframe elixir scripts/run_livemd.exs notebooks/geoframe.livemd
> 
> The setup cell fails with a clear message when the variable is missing. The first run builds
> GeoFrame's Rust core as well as Spatex's (a few minutes in debug mode).

```elixir
geoframe_path =
  System.get_env("SPATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH") ||
    raise "set SPATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH to a GeoFrame checkout before starting this notebook"

Mix.install([
  {:spatex, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:rustler, "~> 0.38", runtime: false},
  {:geoframe, path: geoframe_path},
  {:explorer, "~> 0.13"},
  {:jason, "~> 1.4"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
])
```

## What this notebook does

`Spatex.GeoFrame` connects the two libraries without a compile-time dependency: it builds spatial
graphs straight from a `GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame`, hands attribute columns to the ESDA functions, and
writes the per-site results back as new columns. The analysis is the one in `notebooks/tour.livemd`
(Columbus crime: weights, Moran's I, LISA, G_Local with FDR, classification), driven from a
GeoDataFrame and drawn as deck.gl choropleths with `GeoFrame.Kino.deck/2`.

The Columbus polygons are in arbitrary digitizing units, not longitude and latitude, so the basemap
shows them somewhere off the coast of West Africa. Only their shape matters here.

## A GeoDataFrame from the Columbus data

```elixir
fixture = Path.join([__DIR__, "..", "test", "reference", "fixtures", "datasets", "columbus.json"])
columbus = fixture |> File.read!() |> Jason.decode!()

wkbs = Enum.map(columbus["wkb_hex"], &Base.decode16!(&1, case: :mixed))
attributes = columbus["attributes"]

frame =
  GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.new(
    Explorer.DataFrame.new(crime: attributes["CRIME"], inc: attributes["INC"], hoval: attributes["HOVAL"]),
    GeoFrame.GeoSeries.from_wkb!(wkbs)
  )

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(frame, color: {"crime", :viridis}, opacity: 0.8, tooltip: ["crime", "inc", "hoval"])
```

## Weights and Moran's I

`Spatex.GeoFrame.graph/3` takes the method (`:queen`, `:rook`, `:knn`, `:distance_band`, `:kernel`
or `:triangulation`) and its options. `values/2` returns a column as an `Explorer.Series`, which
every Spatex function accepts.

```elixir
graph = Spatex.GeoFrame.graph(frame, :queen)
crime = Spatex.GeoFrame.values(frame, "crime")

moran = Spatex.ESDA.moran(crime, graph, permutations: 999, seed: 12345)
%{i: moran.i, z: moran.z_norm, p_sim: moran.p_sim}
```

## LISA clusters as columns

`put_results/3` adds the result's per-site fields as columns (`lisa_is`, `lisa_q`, `lisa_p_sim`,
`lisa_cluster`, ...), so the frame keeps geometry, attributes and statistics together.

```elixir
lisa = Spatex.ESDA.moran_local(crime, graph, permutations: 999, seed: 12345)
frame = Spatex.GeoFrame.put_results(frame, lisa)

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(frame,
  color: {"lisa_cluster", :categorical},
  opacity: 0.85,
  tooltip: ["crime", "lisa_cluster", "lisa_p_sim"]
)
```

## Hot spots with FDR

`g_local/3` results go back the same way. The FDR cut-off from `Spatex.ESDA.fdr/2` decides which
sites to call hot or cold; here it is written as a column of its own.

```elixir
gi = Spatex.ESDA.g_local(crime, graph, star: true, permutations: 999, seed: 12345)
cutoff = Spatex.ESDA.fdr(gi.p_sim, 0.05)

frame = Spatex.GeoFrame.put_results(frame, gi)

hot_cold =
  Enum.zip_with(gi.zs, gi.p_sim, fn z, p ->
    cond do
      p >= cutoff -> "not significant"
      z > 0 -> "hot spot"
      true -> "cold spot"
    end
  end)

frame = GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.put(frame, "hot_cold", Explorer.Series.from_list(hot_cold))

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(frame, color: {"hot_cold", :categorical}, tooltip: ["crime", "hot_cold"])
```

## Classification

```elixir
frame = Spatex.GeoFrame.put_results(frame, Spatex.Classify.fisher_jenks(crime, k: 5))

GeoFrame.Kino.deck(frame, color: {"class_yb", :viridis}, tooltip: ["crime", "class_yb"])
```

## Regression with the frame's columns

`Spatex.Regression.ols/3` takes an `Explorer.DataFrame` of regressors and `Explorer.Series`
columns, so the frame's attribute table plugs in directly.

```elixir
x = frame |> GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame.data() |> Explorer.DataFrame.select(["inc", "hoval"])

model =
  Spatex.Regression.ols(crime, x,
    name_y: "crime",
    graph: Spatex.Graph.transform(graph, :r),
    spat_diag: true,
    moran: true
  )

IO.puts(Spatex.Regression.summary(model))
```
